<h2>I. A Quiet Infiltration Underway</h2>

<p>In the fall of 2025, OpenAI announced a partnership with <strong>400,000 U.S. teachers</strong> to shape the future of AI in schools. The same week, a high school student wrote in <em>The Atlantic</em> that &quot;AI Is Demolishing My Education.&quot; Professors told <em>The Guardian</em> they &quot;wish they could push ChatGPT off a cliff.&quot; Meanwhile, <em>Scientific American</em> ran the story of a mother who used vibe coding to build an AI tutor for her dyslexic son — with results that surpassed every human tutor she had hired.</p>

<p>This is what AI integration into America's education system actually looks like in 2026: <strong>not one unified narrative, but infiltration at different speeds, forms, and intentions across every corner of the ecosystem.</strong></p>

<p>The data backs this up. Brookings' latest research on generative AI in tutoring (May 2026) found significant positive effects — but only when teachers were involved in designing and implementing the AI tools. On the other side, student AI adoption rates now exceed 90% (DemandSage, 2026), while the majority of schools still lack a formal AI use policy.</p>

<p><strong>The defining characteristic of this infiltration: users at the bottom (students) have fully embraced AI, while institutions at the top (schools, teachers, policymakers) are still debating whether AI is good or bad.</strong></p>

<h2>II. Three Paths Into the Classroom</h2>

<h3>Path One: Platform Down — Khan Academy's Khanmigo</h3>

<p>Khan Academy's AI tutor Khanmigo, honed over two years, is now being piloted in multiple U.S. school districts. Its design principle: &quot;guide, don't give answers.&quot; When a student asks a math problem, Khanmigo walks through questions rather than providing the answer directly. <em>60 Minutes</em> dedicated a feature to it.</p>

<p>But a deep-dive by <em>Chalkbeat</em> (May 2026) exposed the limitations: AI tutors remain helpless when faced with real classroom behavior issues — zoning out, acting out, emotional breakdowns. <strong>AI excels at &quot;knowledge transfer,&quot; but a classroom has never been just about knowledge transfer.</strong></p>

<h3>Path Two: Resistance and Retreat — South Korea's Cautionary Tale</h3>

<p>The most instructive global reference point is South Korea. In 2025, the Korean government launched an ambitious AI digital textbook initiative — and met with fierce resistance from teachers' unions. Korea's Board of Audit and Inspection ultimately ruled the plan &quot;riddled with serious procedural flaws,&quot; and by late 2025 the government had withdrawn its AI textbook rollout.</p>

<p><em>Business Insider</em> commented: <strong>&quot;South Korea's teacher revolt is a warning for the U.S.&quot;</strong> Korea's case demonstrates that when AI is top-down &quot;force-fed&quot; into an education system, no matter how good the technology, it meets institutional resistance.</p>

<h3>Path Three: Parental Self-Help — Grassroots AI Education Experiments</h3>

<p>The most unexpected path comes from parents. <em>Scientific American</em> profiled Arlyn Gajilan: a news industry professional, not a technologist, whose son Tobey repeatedly broke down over his dyslexia. Using GPTs, she built a custom AI tutor. She told the AI: &quot;You are a special education teacher with expertise in dyslexia,&quot; then fed it Tobey's assessments, evaluations, and favorite interests (dragons from Wings of Fire, Nerf battles). The result? Tobey, for the first time, voluntarily wrote a story — a complex narrative about dragon warfare, with sophisticated sentence structures.</p>

<p><strong>This path reveals something profound: when the formal system fails, AI gives individuals the ability to bypass the system entirely.</strong></p>

<h2>III. The Core Insight: Education Is Being &quot;LangChain-ified&quot;</h2>

<p>Putting these three paths together reveals a structural shift:</p>

<p>Traditional education is a <strong>monolithic black box</strong> — curriculum design, teaching, grading, tutoring, testing, college counseling — all performed by one institution. AI is taking this black box and <strong>splitting it into microservices</strong>:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Knowledge delivery → LLM + RAG systems</li>
  <li>Grading → AI scoring engines</li>
  <li>Individual tutoring → AI tutor bots</li>
  <li>Curriculum design → AI course generators</li>
  <li>College counseling → AI advisory systems</li>
</ul>

<p>These &quot;microservices&quot; were previously inseparable because only human teachers could perform all functions simultaneously. AI allows each to function independently. <strong>The education value chain is being decoupled.</strong></p>

<p>Khanmigo de-coupled &quot;tutoring.&quot; OpenAI's 400K teacher partnerships de-coupled &quot;lesson planning.&quot; Scientific American's mother didn't need any institution — she assembled her own education system.</p>

<p><strong>This is reminiscent of what decentralized finance did to banking a decade ago: not replacing the bank, but pulling the bank's functions apart one by one on top of a peer-to-peer architecture. Education is undergoing the same process.</strong></p>

<h2>IV. The Real Challenge Isn't Technology — It's Redefining the Human Role</h2>

<p>The anxiety of South Korean teachers and American educators shares the same root: <strong>AI has entered teachers' territory, but the boundary of their role has not been redefined.</strong></p>

<p>I asked a friend who teaches at an American public middle school: &quot;Do you think your job will change with AI?&quot; He said: &quot;Less time grading and creating lesson plans, but the same amount of time dealing with student emotional issues and parent communication.&quot;</p>

<p>This answer hits the core: <strong>What AI can replace is &quot;information-processing&quot; teaching labor. What it cannot replace is &quot;social-emotional&quot; labor.</strong></p>

<p>Geoffrey Hinton has repeatedly stated: AI has no consciousness, no intent, no real understanding. The most valuable part of education — motivating an unwilling child, calming an anxious parent, discovering a student's unique gift — requires exactly the kind of human intuition and empathy that AI lacks.</p>

<p><strong>The real question isn't &quot;Will AI replace teachers?&quot; It's &quot;When AI takes over 60% of repetitive tasks, are the remaining 40% worth doing — and can we do them well?&quot;</strong></p>

<h2>V. What This Means for China</h2>

<p>Bringing the lens back to China's perspective:</p>

<p><strong>First</strong>, America's AI education shift is moving from &quot;top-down design&quot; to &quot;grassroots infiltration.&quot; OpenAI's teacher partnership is influencing curriculum design. Khanmigo's district coverage is expanding. Even parents without technical backgrounds can bypass the system.</p>

<p><strong>Second</strong>, &quot;forced top-down&quot; approaches face institutional resistance. Korea's lesson is clear: large-scale AI education policy without deep teacher consultation is doomed.</p>

<p><strong>Third</strong>, for Chinese edtech companies looking to go global: the competitive moat isn't AI capability alone — it's whether your product can embed itself into America's &quot;social-emotional school network.&quot; The AI tutoring function itself is no longer a differentiator (ChatGPT already has it). What gets into schools is a product that helps teachers solve problems, not one that replaces them.</p>

<p><strong>As AI begins to penetrate the most conservative and institutionalized system in society, we may be witnessing not just a technological upgrade of education, but a restructuring of education's production relations. The speed, direction, and cost of this restructuring will determine how the next generation learns and grows in the AI era.</strong></p>

<hr class="article-divider">

<p><em>📍 humanaifit insight: Education may be the most important application of Human-AI Fit — not because AI can teach better, but because human teachers can finally return to their true calling of &quot;nurturing&quot; while leaving &quot;instruction&quot; to the machine. What this requires is not technological capability, but the courage to redefine the role of educator.</em></p>

